\section{Conservation of Groundwater Flow}

\subsection{Darcy's Law}

Darcy's Law describes the flow of water through a porous medium, typically a sediment or volcanic rock formation known as the \textit{aquifer}. Our primary focus is \textit{saturated} aquifers, where the pore spaces between grains are fully filled with water. In this context, groundwater flow is driven by gravity acting on the water within the porous medium and any external forces applied to the water. Assuming the flow is \textit{laminar}—meaning water moves in parallel layers without mixing—we can model the velocity of an individual layer as proportional to the negative pressure gradient: \begin{equation} \bv{u}_d = -K \nabla p, \label{lar} \end{equation} where $\bv{u}_d$ represents Darcy velocity, $K$ is the hydraulic conductivity, and $p$ denotes hydrostatic pressure. \footnote{In some contexts, the hydraulic gradient $\bv{u}_g = -\nabla h$ is employed instead of the pressure gradient $\nabla p$. The two are related, and in a saturated aquifer free of external forces, the hydraulic gradient equals the gravitational acceleration acting on the fluid. Consequently, the laminar flow condition can be expressed as $\bv{u}_d = K \bv{u}_g$.} Although we will provide a non-rigorous justification for this relationship, a rigorous derivation can be found in \cite{Yeh2003}.

